<section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="glossary-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
  <h1 id="glossary-title">Glossary</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Short, plain meanings for the words you will meet in notifications, plans, research papers and local
    conversation about Mukundara. Where a term has a legal meaning, the source is named.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="legal-terms-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Law and Administration</p>
    <h2 id="legal-terms-heading">Legal and planning terms</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-grid">
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Tiger reserve</h3><p>An area notified by a State under section 38V of the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972, on NTCA advice. Despite the name, it protects the whole habitat and all its species.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Core / critical tiger habitat (CTH)</h3><p>The part of a tiger reserve to be kept inviolate for wildlife. In Mukundara: 690.71 sq km since the 5 October 2023 notification.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Buffer</h3><p>The surrounding area where people live and work and where conservation is balanced with livelihoods. In Mukundara: 445.08 sq km since 2023.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Eco-Sensitive Zone (ESZ)</h3><p>A regulated band around a protected area, notified by MoEFCC. Mukundara's ESZ (S.O. 4268(E), 2020) is 248.70 sq km and 0–1 km wide; it is not part of the reserve's area.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Zonal Master Plan (ZMP)</h3><p>The State plan that puts ESZ rules into practice, listing land uses, villages and controls. Mukundara's ZMP was published on 7 December 2023.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Tiger Conservation Plan (TCP)</h3><p>The ten-year management plan required by section 38V. It covers habitat, water, protection, people and monitoring.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>National park and sanctuary</h3><p>Protected areas under the Wild Life (Protection) Act. A national park allows no rights inside; a sanctuary can allow some regulated rights. Mukundara's core grew out of both.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Reserved and protected forest</h3><p>Forest categories under forest law (आरक्षित वन and रक्षित वन in Rajasthan notifications). Reserved forest carries stronger restrictions than protected forest.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Revenue land</h3><p>Land recorded with the revenue department rather than the forest department. Some revenue land was included in the reserve's 2023 schedules.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Gazette notification</h3><p>The official published order that creates or changes a legal boundary or rule. A map, plan or press note does not change a boundary; a notification does.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>NTCA</h3><p>National Tiger Conservation Authority, the statutory body under MoEFCC that oversees tiger reserves and approves their plans and funding.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>MEE</h3><p>Management Effectiveness Evaluation: NTCA's periodic independent assessment of how well each tiger reserve is managed.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>APO</h3><p>Annual Plan of Operations: the yearly budget and work plan that NTCA sanctions for a reserve.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Eco-development committee (EDC)</h3><p>A village-level committee that works with the Forest Department on livelihoods and reducing dependence on the forest.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Voluntary village relocation</h3><p>A government package that lets families living inside the core choose to move out with compensation. It must be voluntary.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Ex-gratia</h3><p>Government compensation paid for loss of life, injury, livestock or crops caused by wild animals.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="eco-terms-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ecology</p>
    <h2 id="eco-terms-heading">Ecological terms</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-grid">
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Dry deciduous forest</h3><p>Forest whose trees shed leaves in the dry season. Mukundara falls under Champion and Seth's “Dry Deciduous Forest” group, dominated by dhok.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Habitat connectivity / corridor</h3><p>Links of forest, scrub, ravine or river valley that let animals and plants move between protected areas, keeping populations genetically healthy.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Pinch period</h3><p>The hot, dry weeks before the monsoon when water and fodder are scarcest and wildlife is most stressed.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Edge effect</h3><p>Changes at the boundary between forest and farms or roads: more grazing, noise, fire and conflict. A long, narrow reserve like Mukundara has a lot of edge.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Riparian</h3><p>Belonging to river banks: the trees, reeds and wildlife that live along flowing water.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Invasive species</h3><p>Introduced plants or animals that spread and crowd out native ones, such as <em>Prosopis juliflora</em> or <em>Lantana camara</em>.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Key Biodiversity Area (KBA)</h3><p>A site identified under a global standard as important for biodiversity, for example some wetlands near Kota.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>WPA schedules (2022)</h3><p>Since the 2022 amendment the Wild Life (Protection) Act has four schedules: I and II for animals (I the strictest), III for plants and IV for CITES-listed specimens.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>IUCN Red List category</h3><p>A global extinction-risk rating such as Least Concern, Vulnerable, Endangered or Critically Endangered. It is separate from Indian legal protection.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Research Grade</h3><p>An iNaturalist data-quality label meaning the community agrees on an identification. It is not a population count.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="local-terms-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Local Words</p>
    <h2 id="local-terms-heading">Local and Hindi terms</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-grid">
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Dhok (धोक)</h3><p><em>Anogeissus pendula</em>, the dominant tree of the Mukundara hills, adapted to thin, rocky soil.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Khoh (खोह)</h3><p>A gorge or deep cleft in the hills, often holding shade, seepage water and old shrines.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Nala / nallah (नाला)</h3><p>A seasonal stream that flows in the monsoon and dries up later in the year.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Talab, talai (तालाब, तलाई)</h3><p>A pond or small reservoir. Many are old village water bodies used by both people and wildlife.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Chowki / naka (चौकी, नाका)</h3><p>A forest post or checkpoint used by forest staff for patrolling and protection.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Hunting mala</h3><p>The Tiger Conservation Plan's term for the raised hunting towers and platforms of the princely era, some still standing in the hills.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Palash (पलाश)</h3><p><em>Butea monosperma</em>, “flame of the forest”, whose orange flowers mark late winter.</p></article>
    <article class="glossary-item"><h3>Spelling variants</h3><p>Mukundara is also written Mukundra or Mukandra; Bhainsrodgarh as Bhainsrorgarh or Bhainsarogarh; Alania as Alniya or Aalaniya; Darrah as Dara. This site uses Mukundara, Bhainsrodgarh, Alania and Darrah.</p></article>
  </div>
